1 INQUIRE

   Asks the terminal for a symbol's value.

   Format

     INQUIRE symbol-name [prompt-string]

2 Parameters

 symbol-name

   The symbol to assign.

 prompt-string

   The prompt, the rest of the line.  Without one, the symbol's name.

2 Description

   INQUIRE prompts with the prompt string and ": ".  The reply has its
   symbols substituted ('SYMBOL'), and is then read as a string
   assignment's value is: uppercased, its blanks compressed, and quoted
   text kept as it is.  Ctrl/Z gives the symbol "".  In a command
   procedure INQUIRE still reads the terminal, not the procedure's data,
   and with SET VERIFY the reply, substituted, is shown on the
   procedure's output.

2 Qualifiers
/GLOBAL

   Makes the symbol global.

/LOCAL

   Makes the symbol local to the command level (the default).

/[NO]PUNCTUATION

   /NOPUNCTUATION leaves out the ": " after the prompt.

2 Examples

   1.$ INQUIRE ANSWER "Continue (Y/N)"
     $ IF .NOT. ANSWER THEN EXIT

     Asks "Continue (Y/N): ", and exits unless the reply starts with Y.
